Feature: Validate delivery_dt Data Standardization in f_order Table
  Ensuring that the delivery_dt column in the f_order table is of type Decimal(38,0) 
  and adheres to the yyyymmdd format as part of the data transition process.

  Background:
    Given the SQL script "/Workspace/stg/scripts/f_order.sql" is available
    And the f_order table exists in the purgo_databricks.purgo_playground catalog

  Scenario Outline: Validate delivery_dt datatype and format
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then the delivery_dt field should be of datatype Decimal(38,0)
    And the delivery_dt values should follow the yyyymmdd format

    Examples:
      | delivery_dt        | expected_result | error_message                   |
      | 20240910           | Pass             |                                  |
      | 2024-09-10         | Fail             | "delivery_dt format invalid"    |
      | 202409100000000000 | Fail             | "delivery_dt exceeds precision"  |
      | NULL               | Fail             | "delivery_dt cannot be NULL"     |
      | 20241301           | Fail             | "delivery_dt contains invalid date" |
      | 20240230           | Fail             | "delivery_dt contains invalid date" |
      | abcdefgh           | Fail             | "delivery_dt contains non-numeric characters" |

  Scenario: All delivery_dt values are valid Decimal(38,0) and follow yyyymmdd format
    Given the f_order table has delivery_dt values as Decimal(38,0)
    And all delivery_dt values are in the yyyymmdd format
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then no validation errors should be found

  Scenario: delivery_dt contains values not in Decimal(38,0) datatype
    Given the f_order table has delivery_dt values with incorrect datatypes
      | delivery_dt       |
      | '20240910'        |
      | '2024-09-10'      |
      | 20240910.00        |
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then the validation should fail
    And the following error messages should be logged:
      | error_message                         |
      | "delivery_dt datatype mismatch"       |
      | "delivery_dt datatype mismatch"       |
      | "delivery_dt datatype mismatch"       |

  Scenario: delivery_dt contains values not following yyyymmdd format
    Given the f_order table has delivery_dt values not in yyyymmdd format
      | delivery_dt   |
      | 2024-9-10     |
      | 2024091       |
      | 24-09-10      |
      | 2024/09/10    |
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then the validation should fail
    And the following error messages should be logged:
      | error_message                     |
      | "delivery_dt format invalid"      |
      | "delivery_dt format invalid"      |
      | "delivery_dt format invalid"      |
      | "delivery_dt format invalid"      |

  Scenario: delivery_dt contains NULL values
    Given the f_order table has NULL values in delivery_dt column
      | delivery_dt |
      | NULL        |
      | NULL        |
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then the validation should fail
    And the following error messages should be logged:
      | error_message                 |
      | "delivery_dt cannot be NULL"  |
      | "delivery_dt cannot be NULL"  |

  Scenario: delivery_dt contains invalid dates in yyyymmdd format
    Given the f_order table has delivery_dt values with invalid dates
      | delivery_dt |
      | 20241301    |
      | 20240230    |
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then the validation should fail
    And the following error messages should be logged:
      | error_message                             |
      | "delivery_dt contains invalid date"       |
      | "delivery_dt contains invalid date"       |

  Scenario: delivery_dt contains non-numeric characters
    Given the f_order table has delivery_dt values with non-numeric characters
      | delivery_dt |
      | abcdefgh    |
      | 2024ab10    |
    When the SQL validation logic is executed
    Then the validation should fail
    And the following error messages should be logged:
      | error_message                                     |
      | "delivery_dt contains non-numeric characters"      |
      | "delivery_dt contains non-numeric characters"      |
